<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Morean War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1684">1684</a>–<a href="/years/1699">1699</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Sixth Ottoman–Venetian War · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-turkish-war">Great Turkish War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Morean War was the war of 1684–1699 between the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-turkish-war">Great Turkish War</a>. Venetian forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-morosini">Francesco Morosini</a> conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> (the Morea) between 1685 and 1687 and briefly took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, where a shell exploded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthenon">Parthenon</a> in 1687. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-karlowitz">Treaty of Karlowitz</a> in 1699 confirmed Venice's hold on the Morea, which the Ottomans retook in 1715.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/morean-war.jpg" alt="Morean War" width="320" height="158" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Georg Hautsch II · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Medal_for_the_Venetian_victories_in_the_Morean_War,_G._Hautsch,_Nuremberg,_1687.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> had held islands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> and Ionian seas since the partition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> but lost most of them to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cretan-war-1645-1669">Cretan War</a> of 1645 to 1669 it lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>. During that war the Venetian commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-morosini">Francesco Morosini</a> made contact with the rebellious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maniots">Maniots</a> and in 1659 landed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Morea</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalamata">Kalamata</a>, but he had to return to Crete and the venture failed.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> declared war on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1684, for the only time in the Ottoman-Venetian wars, on news arriving on 25 April of the formation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-league-1684">Holy League</a>. The Austrians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> valued the Venetian navy as a means of keeping the Ottomans from concentrating their forces. In the Senate the war party saw a unique chance for revenge while the Ottomans were occupied against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Venetian fleet left Corfu on 18 July 1684 and took the fortress of Lefkada after sixteen days, on 6 August. In 1685 Morosini turned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maniots">Maniots</a> sought his aid, but the Ottoman serasker Ismail Pasha invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mani">Mani</a> with 10,000 men and forced them to submit. The conquest of the Morea went on through 1687.</p><p>On 21 September 1687 an army of 10,750 men under Königsmarck landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eleusis">Eleusis</a> and the fleet entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piraeus">Piraeus</a>. The Ottoman garrison withdrew to the Acropolis, which the Venetians besieged from 23 to 29 September. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> used the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthenon">Parthenon</a> to store ammunition, and on the evening of 26 September a mortar shell hit it. The explosion killed 300 people and destroyed the roof and most of the walls. The garrison surrendered on condition of transport to Smyrna.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> the Venetians took Sinj in 1686 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knin">Knin</a> in 1688. Morosini was elected Doge on 3 April 1688 but kept his command, and died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nafplio">Nauplia</a> on 16 January 1694. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> recovered, and the Maniot freebooter Limberakis Gerakaris raided Venetian positions from 1689.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-karlowitz">Treaty of Karlowitz</a> of January 1699 confirmed Venetian possession of Kephalonia and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Morea</a> with Aigina, organised as the Kingdom of the Morea in four provinces. The war had cost the peninsula dearly: the first Venetian census counted 86,468 people against some 200,000 before the war. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> did not win the trust of its Greek Orthodox subjects, who had been used to relative autonomy under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, and an Ottoman campaign in 1715 reclaimed the Morea.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Morean War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During the <b>Morean War</b> the Venetians under Francesco Morosini besieged the Acropolis, where the Ottomans used the Parthenon as a gunpowder magazine and as a shelter.</blockquote><a href="/work/parthenon">Parthenon · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>It came at the start of the <b>Morean War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-7">7 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>Morean War</b>, part of the Great Turkish War, the Venetian commander Francesco Morosini conquered the Peloponnese between 1685 and 1687; during his campaign in Attica a Venetian shell exploded the Parthenon.</blockquote><a href="/state/venetian-morea">Venetian Morea</a></li></ul></section>
